* rdlnecho.a -- read ONE line from standard input with a deliberately large* count, then write back exactly the bytes that I$ReadLn returned.** This is an instrument, not a conformance test, and it is deliberately NOT* in test/68k-conformance/CMDS: it reads standard input, so it could never* run unattended in that suite's runall. It exists because nothing on a v2.4* system disk asks for more than 512 bytes on a console read -- measured, by* `-d 2` trace: the shell asks $200, BASIC09 $1FF, bash/mshell/kermit/vi/* umacs all $200 or less -- so no shipped program can tell a system that* caps a console I$ReadLn from one that does not.** What the caller sees is the line itself, not a number this module reports* about itself, so there is nothing to trust: feed it a line longer than the* cap and count what comes back.** Nothing here links against a C library or a trap handler, exactly like the* conformance suite: a kernel and a shell are enough to run it.Prgrm     set 1Objct     set 1ReEnt     set $80Type_Lang set (Prgrm<<8)+ObjctAttr_Rev  set (ReEnt<<8)+1IReadLn   set $8BIWrite    set $8AFExit     set $06** Twice the 512 bytes SCF is documented to allocate for line editing, so one* run separates "honours the caller's count" from "capped at the buffer".ASKED     set 1024* psect rdlnecho,Type_Lang,Attr_Rev,0,8192,startstart* Buffer on the stack, never in the code psect: this module is marked* re-entrant and a real system may hold it in ROM, where a write into its* own body would fault rather than just be untidy. sub.l #ASKED,a7 movea.l a7,a4 movea.l a4,a0 move.l #ASKED,d1 moveq #0,d0 trap #0 dc.w IReadLn bcs.s done* d1 still carries the count I$ReadLn returned; write back precisely that* many bytes. I$Write, not I$WritLn, so the output is not re-edited on the* way out and the byte count the reader sees is the one the read produced. movea.l a4,a0 moveq #1,d0 trap #0 dc.w IWritedone moveq #0,d1 trap #0 dc.w FExit ends